PROJECT_NAME uses two licenses.

1. CONTENT (course text, slides, labs, guides, forms, images):
   Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0).
   Full text: LICENSES/CC-BY-4.0.txt
   https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

2. CODE (scripts, sample programs, config files):
   Apache License, Version 2.0.
   Full text: LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt
   https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0

WHY TWO LICENSES
Software licenses are written for code, and they fit training content poorly.
CC BY 4.0 is made for creative and educational work: anyone may copy, translate,
adapt, and teach from it, even commercially, as long as they give credit.
Apache-2.0 is a well-known software license that also includes a patent grant,
which matters for code that people will run and build on.

If a file is code, Apache-2.0 applies. Everything else is CC BY 4.0.
How to give credit: "Based on PROJECT_NAME (link to this repository), CC BY 4.0."
